<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Arabia and the Gulf</p><h1 class="st-title">Sulayhid dynasty</h1><p class="st-meta">1047 CE – 1138 CE · capital Sanaa</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">An Isma'ili dynasty that united Yemen under the Fatimid banner and is remembered for Queen Arwa, one of the few women to rule in medieval Islam.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ali-ibn-muhammad-al-sulayhi">Ali ibn Muhammad al-Sulayhi</a>, a judge's son and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ismailism">Isma'ili</a> missionary for the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/fatimid-caliphate">Fatimid Caliphate</a>, rose in revolt in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haraz-mountains">Haraz</a> mountains in 1047. Within two decades he had taken Sanaa, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zabid">Zabid</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aden">Aden</a> and brought most of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/republic-of-yemen">Yemen</a> under his rule, recognising the Fatimid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The Sulayhids spread the Fatimid mission to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oman">Oman</a> and western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. After Ali was killed by the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/najahid-dynasty">Najahid dynasty</a>, power passed to his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-al-mukarram">al-Mukarram Ahmad</a> and then to Ahmad's wife, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arwa-al-sulayhi">Arwa bint Ahmad</a>, who governed from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jibla">Dhu Jibla</a> for some fifty years, built its great mosque and was given authority over the Fatimid mission in <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/republic-of-yemen">Yemen</a>. Governors such as the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/zurayid-dynasty">Zurayids</a> ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aden">Aden</a> for them.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>When the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatimid-caliphate">Fatimids</a> split over succession in 1130, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arwa-al-sulayhi">Arwa</a> backed the infant al-Tayyib, founding the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tayyibi-ismailism">Tayyibi</a> branch of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ismailism">Isma'ilism</a>. After her death in 1138 the state broke apart among the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/zurayid-dynasty">Zurayid dynasty</a>, the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/hamdanid-sultanate-of-yemen">Hamdanid sultanate of Yemen</a> and others. Tayyibi leadership later moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, where the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohras">Bohras</a> continue it.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/sulayhid-dynasty.jpg" alt="Sulayhid dynasty" width="320" height="153"><figcaption>Image: H. Grobe · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jemen1988-022_hg.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1047 CE – 1138 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Sanaa</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Ali ibn Muhammad al-Sulayhi, al-Mukarram Ahmad, Arwa al-Sulayhi</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Isma'ili Shi'a Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Arabic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/yemen">Yemen</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Sulayhids, Sulayhid state</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1092&amp;focus=sulayhid-dynasty" aria-label="Sulayhid dynasty on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5887 1607 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Sulayhid dynasty lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6207 1863L6186 1905M6207 1863L5811 1337M6207 1863L6236 1957M6207 1863L6207 1863"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6186 1905h0M5811 1337h0M6236 1957h0M6207 1863h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6207 1863h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6207 1863h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Sanaa. <a href="/atlas?year=1092&amp;focus=sulayhid-dynasty">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

